---
/**
 * Act I, section 02: `stacktape deploy`.
 *
 * The transcript sits beside a four-phase read of what it is doing, because the argument of this
 * section is not "there is a nice terminal" — it is that one command owns packaging, the change
 * set, the rollout and the outputs, and tells you which of them it is in.
 *
 * The hot-swap beat is the caption. The surface already renders the second deploy under the
 * transcript; the caption says why it is there rather than repeating what it says.
 */
import { DeployTerminal } from '../../surfaces/DeployTerminal';
import SectionHead from './SectionHead.astro';
---

<section class="b-band b-band--deep" id="s02" data-act="0">
  <div class="b-shell">
    <SectionHead act="0" index="02" kicker="stacktape deploy">
      <Fragment slot="title">One command carries the whole pipeline.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="lede">
        Packaging, the CloudFormation change set, the rollout and the outputs. It is your account and your stack the
        whole way — Stacktape is the thing that wrote the template and is watching it apply.
      </Fragment>
    </SectionHead>

    <div class="b-figure b-split b-reveal">
      <figure class="b-figure b-figure--flush">
        <div class="b-figure__stage b-figure__stage--bare">
          <DeployTerminal />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="b-figure__caption">
          <b>The panel under the transcript is the next deploy.</b> When only your code changed, Stacktape skips
          CloudFormation and swaps the artifact in seconds — so the three minutes above is a first deploy, not a
          habit.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <ol class="b-notes">
        <li>
          <h3><i>01</i> Initialize</h3>
          <p>Resolves the config, the stage and the AWS account, and diffs what is already deployed.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3><i>02</i> Package</h3>
          <p>
            Builds every function and container image, with shared dependencies lifted into a Lambda layer. Measured at
            roughly <b>7× faster than AWS CDK</b> on 15 Lambdas with shared dependencies.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3><i>03</i> Deploy</h3>
          <p>
            One CloudFormation change set, applied and reported resource by resource. <code>c</code> cancels and rolls
            back; a failed stack rolls itself back the way CloudFormation always has.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3><i>04</i> Outputs</h3>
          <p>The URLs, the connection details, and a link into the Console — which is where Act II starts.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
